<title>SPAIN: Spain, Venezuela stock markets top for 1996.</title>
<headline>Spain, Venezuela stock markets top for 1996.</headline>
<dateline>MADRID 1996-12-27</dateline>
<text>
<p>The Spanish stock market was the biggest gainer for 1996 outside emerging markets, while Venezuela outperformed all others with a 120 percent rise, new data from Morgan Stanley Capital International showed on Friday.</p>
<p>The data, provided to Reuters, calculated returns as of Friday in U.S. dollar terms.</p>
<p>Hungary came in a close second to Venezuela with a nearly 102 percent rise followed by Poland, up 55.5 percent, then Taiwan, China and Brazil, which were each up 36 to 38 percent.</p>
<p>Spain led non-emerging markets with a 34 percent gain followed by Sweden, Portugal, Finland, Canada, Ireland and the United States.</p>
<p>Thailand was the big loser, down almost 38 percent for the year, a setback closely matched by South Korea. South Africa, Sri Lanka, Chile and Japan were also among the worst performers.</p>
<p>-- Madrid Newsroom +341-585-2160</p>
</text>
